// Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon — the `__gt_release_<callee>` helpers that discharge a green thread's
// ownership of its managed ARGUMENTS and of its managed RESULT-or-ERROR (P1.5-B1c A2).
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A PER-CALLEE FUNCTION AND NOT A BITMASK IN THE GT STRUCT.** A mask that said "slot 0 and slot 2
// are managed" would have to be released by ONE runtime entry, and there is no such entry: a release in shv2
// is TYPE-DIRECTED, and `Parser.decrefCalleeFor` routes to seven of them (`__str_decref`,
// `__destruct_<Struct>`, `__destruct_<mangled>`, `__drop_type_param`, `__drop_existential`, `__mm_decref`,
// `__gt_promise_drop`). The generic `__mm_decref` is not a safe default for the rest: `decrefCalleeFor`'s own
// `tagIsByteRecord` arm states what happens when a String reaches it — it *"frees the box without releasing
// the external buffer a detached record owns"*, a leak on exactly the arguments this file exists to release.
// A mask can name one callee; a spawn's slots can want three.
//
// ⭐ **AND WHY THE RELEASE CANNOT SIMPLY BE EMITTED AT THE AWAIT.** The parser does know the types — but
// `__gt_await` RECYCLES the GT struct onto the free list before returning (`emitGtStructReclaim`), and
// `buildGtSpawn`'s header records what a recycled struct still carries: *"its previous tenant's
// status/result/waiter/io_*/threw/tib"*. Reading argument slots after the awaiter has returned reads a
// struct that may already have been re-let. So the release happens INSIDE the runtime, while the GT is still
// this promise's, and the only thing the spawn has to leave behind is the address of the code that knows how.
//
// ⭐ **DEDUP IS BY CALLEE, AND THE ARGUMENT TYPES ARE THE CALLEE'S SIGNATURE.** Two spawns of `joinArgs` pass
// the same parameter types by definition, so there is exactly one release plan per async callee and the whole
// plan is DERIVED from `funcSignatures` + `ProgramSignatures` at synthesis time — nothing is recorded at the
// spawn site and nothing can drift between the site and the body. This mirrors `Runtime/FnRefThunk.maxon`,
// the tree's other per-callee synthesis, down to the collect-early / install-post-pipeline shape.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO CALL SITES ARE ASYMMETRIC ABOUT THE RESULT, AND COLLAPSING THEM INTO ONE IS THE OBVIOUS NEXT
// BUG.** The choice travels as the two-case `AsyncResultDisposition` rather than as a bare 1 or 0 for exactly
// that reason — see that union, which is where the runtime's word for it is chosen:
//
//   • `__gt_await` / `__gt_try_await` pass **`consumedByAwaiter`** — arguments only. The awaited value's `+1`
//     has just been handed to the awaiting frame, which enrols it and drops it once
//     (`Parser.emitAwaitOp`'s `enrolOwnedCallTemp`). **Releasing it here as well is a DOUBLE FREE.**
//   • `__gt_promise_drop` passes **`ownedByThisDrop`** — arguments *and* the result-or-error. No await ever
//     ran, so nobody else will ever release them. **Skipping it here is a LEAK** (exit 101), and it is the
//     only place the leak can be closed. Cancel routes through `promise_drop`, so it inherits this rather
//     than growing a third copy of the decision.
//
// ⚠ **A DOUBLE CALL IS PREVENTED AT COMPILE TIME, AND THAT IS THE PRIMARY MECHANISM.** `Parser.emitAwaitOp`
// calls `consumePromiseThread`, which clears the promise from `pendingTempDrops` and sets `movedFrom` through
// the shared `VarInfo`; the scope-exit / re-arm gate reads `not binding.movedFrom and bindingIsOwned(binding)`,
// so an awaited promise emits no `__gt_promise_drop` at all. **The `gt.releaser = 0` store both call sites
// make is a RUNTIME BACKSTOP, second in line** — one store, an honest state transition ("this GT no longer
// owes a release"), and it composes with cancel, which reaches the same drop entry by another road.
//
// ⚠ **THE THROW PATH HAS TWO TRAPS AND THEY ARE DIFFERENT TRAPS.** On a throw `gt.result` holds whatever R8
// happened to carry out of the throwing exit — the UNDEFINED success slot — so releasing it there is a wild
// free; the success release is therefore gated on `threw == 0`. And `gt.threw` is GARBAGE for a NON-throwing
// callee (`buildGtTrampolineChunk` captures R10 unconditionally and says so), so it must not be read at all
// unless the callee throws. **"the callee throws" and "the error is managed" are two facts here, carried
// separately**, because a throwing callee with a scalar error type has a `threw` worth branching on and
// nothing to release.
//
// ⚠ **CAPACITY IS NOT THIS FILE'S TO BOUND.** `Parser.emitAsyncCall` already refuses `args.count() >
// MaxAsyncArgs` with a positioned diagnostic and `LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncSpawn` keeps a structural panic
// behind it, so a plan can never name a slot past the inline region. (`async-await.managed-args-many` passes
// two arguments and so does not approach that gate — it is a guard against the spawn-site reference being
// missing, not against arity.)
//
// ⭐ **MULTI-M: TWO OF THE THREE STEPS THIS BLOCK USED TO NAME ARE CLOSED (G2), AND THE THIRD IS NAMED
// PRECISELY.**
//   (1) **The `gt.releaser` load / clear pair** was a read-modify-write with the ENTIRE release inside it —
//       two Ms could both observe the same non-null releaser and both run it, which is a DOUBLE FREE of every
//       managed argument the spawn owned, not a lost count. It is a CAS now: whoever wins the swap to 0 is
//       the one that calls. `GtRuntime.emitGtReleaserCall` carries the argument and the no-retry-loop proof.
//   (3) **The refcounts the emitted drop callees touch** were made atomic here and are PLAIN again since
//       EC10 — a releaser runs on the M of the green thread that owns the spawn, which is the only party
//       that ever holds those boxes (`MmRuntime.emitAdjustRefcount`; `SchedRuntime.maxon` states the
//       invariant). What survived the flip back is the half that was doing the work: the decref decides "am
//       I the last owner?" from the value its own decrement displaced rather than from a prior read.
//   (2) **The drop path's `threw`/`result` reads** are the one still open here, and they are a different
//       shape from the other two: `__gt_promise_drop` reads `gt.status`, picks an arm, and then reads
//       `gt.threw`/`gt.result` — while a completion landing on another M can move `status` in between. It is
//       not a lost update (each word is one aligned machine word, written whole) but a torn VIEW across two
//       of them, and closing it means publishing the completion as one transition the drop can test once —
//       which is a GT state-machine change, not an atomic. ⚠ Reaching it needs a promise dropped by one M
//       while another completes its thread, which per-await linearity makes unreachable for `await` and
//       leaves open for `cancel`.

// The symbol prefix. Its `__gt_` head puts a releaser inside the syscall-shim family exactly as the rest of
// the green-thread runtime is, which matters because a drop callee it calls may reach a kernel call.
let AsyncReleaserPrefix = b"__gt_release_"

// The name of the releaser for async callee `callee`. ONE builder, shared by the spawn lowering
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncSpawn`, which emits `funcAddr(__gt_release_<callee>)`) and the synthesis below,
// so the two spellings cannot drift — `fnRefThunkName`'s arrangement, for its reason.
export function asyncReleaserName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(AsyncReleaserPrefix)}{String.from(callee)}".toByteArray()
end 'asyncReleaserName'

// A set of callee names, for O(1) dedup while collecting releaser targets in source order.
export typealias AsyncReleaserSet = Set with ByteArray

// ⛔⛔ **WHO OWNS THE FINISHED THREAD'S RESULT — the releaser's second argument, as a NAME rather than a 1 or
// a 0.** This file's header states what each of the two mistakes costs (a double free one way, a leak the
// other) and that collapsing the two sites is the obvious next bug; a bare `1` and a bare `0` at the two call
// sites is exactly the spelling that invites it, because neither one says which it is and the two are one
// keystroke apart. Each site now names its own answer, and `asyncResultConsumedFlag` is the ONE place the
// runtime's word for it is chosen.
//   • `consumedByAwaiter` — `__gt_await` / `__gt_try_await` have just handed the result's `+1` to the
//     awaiting frame, which drops it once. The releaser must release the ARGUMENTS ONLY.
//   • `ownedByThisDrop` — `__gt_promise_drop` (and `p.cancel()`, which routes through it) is the last reader
//     the result will ever have. The releaser must release the arguments AND the result-or-error.
export union AsyncResultDisposition
	consumedByAwaiter
	ownedByThisDrop
end 'AsyncResultDisposition'

// The word the synthesized releaser tests (`resultConsumed != 0` ⇒ stop after the arguments). Read HERE and
// nowhere else, so the encoding is one fact and the call sites carry only the meaning.
export function asyncResultConsumedFlag(disposition AsyncResultDisposition) returns ParsedInt
	return match disposition 'disposition'
		consumedByAwaiter gives 1
		ownedByThisDrop gives 0
	end 'disposition'
end 'asyncResultConsumedFlag'

// One slot of a spawn that owes a release: the byte offset it occupies inside the GT struct, and the runtime
// entry that drops it. The offset is carried rather than the slot INDEX because the result and the error
// live at fixed struct offsets while the arguments live in the inline region — three sources, one shape, so
// the emitter below has one loop instead of three.
type AsyncManagedSlot
	export let offset as ByteOffset
	export let dropCallee as ByteArray

	export static function create(offset ByteOffset, dropCallee ByteArray) returns AsyncManagedSlot
		return Self{offset: offset, dropCallee: dropCallee}
	end 'create'
end 'AsyncManagedSlot'

export typealias AsyncManagedSlotArray = Array with AsyncManagedSlot

// ⭐ **THE WHOLE RELEASE PLAN FOR ONE ASYNC CALLEE**, derived from its signature. Four independent facts,
// and the reason they are four rather than a flag or two is the throw path: `throws` is what decides whether
// `gt.threw` may be READ at all, and `errorSlots` is what decides whether there is anything to release once
// it has been. A throwing callee with a scalar error type has the first and not the second.
//
// `resultSlots`/`errorSlots` are arrays holding at most one entry each, so that all three lists feed the
// SAME `emitSlotReleases` loop; a `union { none; one(slot) }` would say the same thing and need its own arm
// at every use.
type AsyncReleasePlan
	export let argSlots as AsyncManagedSlotArray
	export let resultSlots as AsyncManagedSlotArray
	export let errorSlots as AsyncManagedSlotArray
	export let calleeThrows as bool

	export static function create(argSlots AsyncManagedSlotArray, resultSlots AsyncManagedSlotArray, errorSlots AsyncManagedSlotArray, calleeThrows bool) returns AsyncReleasePlan
		return Self{argSlots: argSlots, resultSlots: resultSlots, errorSlots: errorSlots, calleeThrows: calleeThrows}
	end 'create'

	// Is there nothing at all to release? Then the spawn writes NOTHING into `gt.releaser`
	// (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncReleaserStore` returns early — the 0 is already there, from the slab zero on
	// a fresh struct and from `__gt_spawn`'s whole-struct memzero on a recycled one), and both release sites
	// read that 0 and SKIP rather than indirect-calling through it.
	//
	// ⚠ **THAT IS "PAYS ALMOST NOTHING", NOT "EMITS NOTHING".** `emitGtReleaserCall` is emitted
	// UNCONDITIONALLY into `__gt_await`, `__gt_try_await` and `__gt_promise_drop` — the compiler cannot know
	// at runtime-build time which callees a program spawns — so every async program gained its load, compare
	// and branch plus the two blocks each of those three carries. A scalar-only async program's emitted code
	// therefore DIFFERS from a pre-A2 build; what it does not do is call anything. See `emitGtReleaserCall`'s
	// own header, which states the same cost from the other end.
	export function isInert() returns bool
		return self.argSlots.count() == 0 and self.resultSlots.count() == 0 and self.errorSlots.count() == 0
	end 'isInert'
end 'AsyncReleasePlan'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS SPAWN NEED A RELEASER? — ONE fact, read by the two sites that must not disagree about it.**
// `LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncSpawn` NAMES the address it stores into `gt.releaser`, and `installAsyncReleasers`
// decides whether anything is there to name.
//
// ⚠ **A DISAGREEMENT IS A COMPILE ERROR AT NEITHER SITE** — `functionValueNeedsEnvThunk`'s trap, one runtime
// over, and worse here. Say-yes at the spawn and no at the synthesis and the program holds a `funcAddr` of a
// releaser nobody built (a link failure, which at least reports). Say-no at the spawn and yes at the
// synthesis and the GT carries 0, every release is skipped and the program LEAKS every managed argument it
// ever spawned with — silently, until the leak gate at exit says 101 with nothing to point at.
export function asyncCalleeNeedsReleaser(callee ByteArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns bool
	return not asyncReleasePlanFor(callee, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames).isInert()
end 'asyncCalleeNeedsReleaser'

// ⭐⭐ **THE REPLY CELL's RELEASER (SV2) — the SAME body, over a DIFFERENT plan.** A cell owes exactly one
// release: the managed RESULT a message answered that nobody ever took, which the second arrival at the
// teardown rendezvous (`__gt_reclaim`) frees with `ownedByThisDrop`. Its symbol is in its own namespace
// because the plans differ in what they name: `__gt_release_<callee>` is the plan of a SPAWN of `<callee>`
// and lists that callee's arguments, while this one lists nothing but the result — so a program that both
// `async`s and message-sends one function would otherwise have two plans fighting over one symbol.
export function serviceReplyReleaserName(messageName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(ServiceReplyReleaserPrefix)}{String.from(messageName)}".toByteArray()
end 'serviceReplyReleaserName'

let ServiceReplyReleaserPrefix = b"__gt_reply_release_"

// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS REPLY NEED A RELEASER? — ONE fact, read by the two sites that must not disagree**, exactly
// as `asyncCalleeNeedsReleaser` is: `LowerMaxonToStd.emitServiceReplyCell` NAMES the address it stores into
// `cell.releaser`, and `installServiceReplyReleasers` decides whether anything is there to name. See
// `asyncCalleeNeedsReleaser` for what each of the two disagreements costs.
export function serviceReplyNeedsReleaser(messageName ByteArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns bool
	return not serviceReplyReleasePlanFor(messageName, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames).isInert()
end 'serviceReplyNeedsReleaser'

// ⭐ **THE PLAN, AND ITS THREE DEPARTURES FROM `asyncReleasePlanFor` ARE THE THREE FACTS ABOUT A CELL.**
//
//   • **NO ARGUMENT SLOTS.** A message's arguments were moved into the request BOX at the send and are
//     released by `<T>.__loop` (consumed by the handler, or dropped slot by slot beside it) — the GT
//     struct's inline argument region is untouched and stays at the slab's zero. Deriving them from the
//     message's parameters, as the async plan does, would name offsets nothing ever wrote.
//   • **NO ERROR SLOT.** A reply's `threw` word carries the FUSED ORDINAL of a scalar dispatch line, never a
//     box: `ServiceCompanions.uncarriableReplyErrorNoun` refuses a message whose `throws` names a
//     payload-carrying union at the `spawn` (E3140), precisely so this word can never be a pointer.
//   • **`calleeThrows` IS UNCONDITIONALLY TRUE**, because a reply ALWAYS throws `ServiceError` whatever the
//     message declares — which is what gates the result release on `threw == 0`, the one reading that keeps
//     a transport failure's zero result slot away from a drop callee.
//
// ⚠ **AN UNDECLARED MESSAGE ANSWERS THE INERT PLAN**, on `asyncReleasePlanFor`'s reason plus one of its own:
// `<T>.shutdown` is the compiler's own pill, which no file declares and which answers nothing, so the
// lookup below failing IS the correct answer for it rather than a doomed program's symptom.
function serviceReplyReleasePlanFor(messageName ByteArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns AsyncReleasePlan
	let noSlots = AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	let sig = try funcSignatures.get(messageName) otherwise return AsyncReleasePlan.create(noSlots, resultSlots: AsyncManagedSlotArray.create(), errorSlots: AsyncManagedSlotArray.create(), calleeThrows: true)
	return AsyncReleasePlan.create(noSlots, resultSlots: asyncResultSlotsOf(messageName, sig: sig, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames), errorSlots: AsyncManagedSlotArray.create(), calleeThrows: true)
end 'serviceReplyReleasePlanFor'

// Every MESSAGE a reply cell is minted for anywhere in the module, in first-seen source order, deduped —
// `collectAsyncSpawnTargets`' twin, over the op that mints a cell rather than the one that spawns a thread.
export function collectServiceReplyTargets(module MaxonModule) returns ByteArrayArray
	var seen = AsyncReleaserSet.create()
	var targets = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("collectServiceReplyTargets: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				noteIfServiceReplyCell(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectServiceReplyTargets: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), seen: seen, targets: targets)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return targets
end 'collectServiceReplyTargets'

function noteIfServiceReplyCell(op MaxonOp, seen AsyncReleaserSet, targets ByteArrayArray)
	match op 'op'
		serviceReplyCell(_, messageName) then noteAsyncCallee(messageName, seen: seen, targets: targets)
		// Every other op mints no reply cell. Two ranges rather than one because `serviceReplyCell` is the
		// union's LAST variant and the rest of the dialect sits below it.
		call to errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		serviceSpawn to serviceSend then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteIfServiceReplyCell'

// The reply releasers' half of `declareAsyncReleaserNeeds` — see there for why a body built after the
// pipeline has to declare what it will call before `noteDestructorUsage` runs its fixpoint.
export function declareServiceReplyReleaserNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage, targets ByteArrayArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner)
	for messageName in targets 'eachMessage'
		declareSlotDropNeeds(usage, slots: serviceReplyReleasePlanFor(messageName, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames).resultSlots)
	end 'eachMessage'
end 'declareServiceReplyReleaserNeeds'

// Install the reply releaser for every message that needs one, through the SAME synthesis the async plans
// go through — the two differ in their plan and in their symbol, and in nothing else.
export function installServiceReplyReleasers(module StdModule, targets ByteArrayArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner)
	for messageName in targets 'eachMessage'
		let plan = serviceReplyReleasePlanFor(messageName, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames)
		if plan.isInert() 'nothingToRelease'
			continue
		end 'nothingToRelease'
		synthesizeReleaser(module, name: serviceReplyReleaserName(messageName), plan: plan)
	end 'eachMessage'
end 'installServiceReplyReleasers'

// Build the release plan for `callee` off its declared signature.
//
// ⚠ **AN UNDECLARED CALLEE ANSWERS THE INERT PLAN RATHER THAN PANICKING.** Its program is already rejected
// by SemanticCheck's E3004, but the collect walk below runs over the module regardless of whether the
// program is doomed, and a panic here would replace a positioned diagnostic with a compiler crash.
function asyncReleasePlanFor(callee ByteArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns AsyncReleasePlan
	var argSlots = AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	let sig = try funcSignatures.get(callee) otherwise return AsyncReleasePlan.create(argSlots, resultSlots: AsyncManagedSlotArray.create(), errorSlots: AsyncManagedSlotArray.create(), calleeThrows: false)

	// The ARGUMENTS, in PARAMETER order — which is the order the inline region holds them in, because
	// `lowerAsyncCall` slots them (`slotCallArgs`) before `emitAsyncSpawn` stores them. A labelled spawn
	// therefore needs no adjustment here: the label was resolved one tier up.
	for i in 0 upto sig.paramCount() 'eachParam'
		let paramType = try sig.paramTypes.get(i) otherwise panic("asyncReleasePlanFor: paramTypes.get OOB at {i}")
		let aggregateName = try sig.paramAggregateNames.get(i) otherwise panic("asyncReleasePlanFor: paramAggregateNames.get OOB at {i} — a signature's param columns are built in lockstep")
		if asyncSlotOwnsHeap(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, slotType: paramType, aggregateName: aggregateName) 'managedArg'
			argSlots.push(AsyncManagedSlot.create((GtOffArgInline + i * GtArgSlotBytes) as ByteOffset, dropCallee: asyncSlotDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, slotType: paramType, aggregateName: aggregateName)))
		end 'managedArg'
	end 'eachParam'

	return AsyncReleasePlan.create(argSlots, resultSlots: asyncResultSlotsOf(callee, sig: sig, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames), errorSlots: asyncErrorSlotsOf(callee, signatures: signatures), calleeThrows: throwsClauseIsThrowing(signatures.throwsOf(callee)))
end 'asyncReleasePlanFor'

// The RESULT slot, if the callee returns a managed value. It reads the return type off the whole-program
// registry (`returnTypeOf`, remapped `into` the interner the rest of this plan is resolved against) and its
// pre-erasure identity off `FuncSignature.returnAggregateName` — the return twin of `paramAggregateNames`,
// and needed for the same reason: a boxed union RETURN resolves to bare `integer` too.
function asyncResultSlotsOf(callee ByteArray, sig FuncSignature, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner) returns AsyncManagedSlotArray
	let returnType = match signatures.returnTypeOf(callee, into: typeNames) 'r'
		declared(declaredReturn) gives declaredReturn
		undeclared gives MaxonReturnType.void
	end 'r'
	return match returnType 'rt'
		void gives AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
		value(inner) gives asyncSlotsAt(GtOffResult as ByteOffset, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames, slotType: inner, aggregateName: sig.returnAggregateName)
	end 'rt'
end 'asyncResultSlotsOf'

// The 0-or-1 slot list for ONE candidate word of the GT struct — the shape every arm of a plan is built
// from, so "a managed slot is a (offset, drop callee) pair decided by `asyncSlotOwnsHeap`" is written once
// and the three arms differ only in which word they are asking about.
function asyncSlotsAt(offset ByteOffset, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, slotType MaxonType, aggregateName ByteArray) returns AsyncManagedSlotArray
	var slots = AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	if asyncSlotOwnsHeap(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, slotType: slotType, aggregateName: aggregateName) 'ownsHeap'
		slots.push(AsyncManagedSlot.create(offset, dropCallee: asyncSlotDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, slotType: slotType, aggregateName: aggregateName)))
	end 'ownsHeap'
	return slots
end 'asyncSlotsAt'

// The ERROR slot, if a throwing callee's error flag is a heap BOX this release must free.
//
// ⭐ **ONLY A BOXED ERROR UNION IS A POINTER**, and that is the whole of the test. A SCALAR error clause
// travels as `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` — a small integer that owns nothing and must never be handed
// to a drop callee — while a boxed union's flag is written to `gt.threw` VERBATIM, with no bias added
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerThrowError`'s boxed arm), so a non-zero flag there IS the box pointer. `enumInAnyFile(…)
// .isBoxed()` is the same question `Parser.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion` asks at the catch site, asked of the same
// registry, so the encode, the catch and this release cannot come to three different answers.
//
// A boxed union with no managed payload still owes a `__mm_decref` — the box itself is heap — which is
// exactly what `unionBoxDropCallee` hands back for it.
function asyncErrorSlotsOf(callee ByteArray, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns AsyncManagedSlotArray
	let errorName = try throwsClauseName(signatures.throwsOf(callee)) otherwise return AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	return match signatures.enumInAnyFile(errorName.toByteArray()) 'e'
		declared(layout) gives asyncBoxedErrorSlots(layout, signatures: signatures, errorName: errorName.toByteArray())
		undeclared gives AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	end 'e'
end 'asyncErrorSlotsOf'

// The error slot for a declared error union — one entry iff the union is BOXED. A non-boxed clause's flag is
// `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, a small integer that owns nothing, and handing it to a drop callee is a
// wild free.
function asyncBoxedErrorSlots(layout EnumLayout, signatures ProgramSignatures, errorName ByteArray) returns AsyncManagedSlotArray
	var slots = AsyncManagedSlotArray.create()
	if layout.isBoxed() 'boxedError'
		slots.push(AsyncManagedSlot.create(GtOffThrew as ByteOffset, dropCallee: signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(errorName)))
	end 'boxedError'
	return slots
end 'asyncBoxedErrorSlots'

// ⭐ **DOES A GT SLOT OF THIS DECLARED TYPE HOLD A HEAP RECORD THE THREAD MUST RELEASE?** The pair below is
// the one classification the whole feature reads: the plan above decides WHICH SLOTS IT DROPS from it, and
// `SemanticCheck.requireAsyncScalarParams` decides WHICH CONSUMED PARAMETERS IT REFUSES from it. A
// disagreement between those two is a double free on one side and a leak on the other, and a compile error
// on neither — so they ask one function.
//
// ⚠ **`aggregateName` IS NOT REDUNDANT WITH `slotType`, AND DROPPING IT LEAKS A BOXED UNION.** Type
// resolution collapses a boxed union to bare `integer` (`TypeResolution.resolveNamedType`, the same erasure
// a ranged alias gets), so a signature's `paramTypes` carries no name for one; the pre-erasure identity
// survives only on `paramAggregateNames`/`returnAggregateName`. Asked of `slotType` alone, a
// `union { ok(String) … }` parameter reads as a scalar and its box is never freed.
//
// ⚠ The boxedness is re-asked of the registry rather than inferred from "the name is non-empty": the
// aggregate column is filled from whatever still tags `named` after resolution, and a slot that owns no box
// must not be handed to a drop callee. One question, one registry.
export function asyncSlotOwnsHeap(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, slotType MaxonType, aggregateName ByteArray) returns bool
	if asyncErasedUnionIsBoxed(signatures, aggregateName: aggregateName) 'erasedBoxedUnion'
		return true
	end 'erasedBoxedUnion'

	return signatures.typeIsManaged(slotType, from: typeNames)
end 'asyncSlotOwnsHeap'

// Which runtime entry drops such a slot. Precondition: `asyncSlotOwnsHeap`. The non-erased arm is the SHARED
// `managedFieldDropCallee` — the same router a managed FIELD of the same type is dropped through and the same
// one `Parser.decrefCalleeFor` routes a top-level owned value through — so a value the spawn took a reference
// to is released by the callee that reference was taken for.
//
// ⚠ **THE INTERNER IS `project.typeNames` AND NOT THE SIGNATURES' OWN.** A `FuncSignature`'s `paramTypes`
// ids are folded into the PROJECT interner by `mergeArtifact` (which is why every SemanticCheck site renders
// them `typeNames: project.typeNames`), while `ProgramSignatures` keeps a separate interner for the layouts
// it owns — which is why the convenience wrappers `slotTypeIsManaged`/`slotTypeDropCallee` cannot serve here.
// Resolving a signature's ids against the wrong interner is OPEN #52's exact shape: it misreads a managed
// payload whenever a ranged alias shifts the interning order, and the symptom is a leak or a wild free.
function asyncSlotDropCallee(signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner, slotType MaxonType, aggregateName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	if asyncErasedUnionIsBoxed(signatures, aggregateName: aggregateName) 'erasedBoxedUnion'
		return signatures.unionBoxDropCallee(aggregateName)
	end 'erasedBoxedUnion'

	return managedFieldDropCallee(signatures, typeNames: typeNames, fieldType: slotType)
end 'asyncSlotDropCallee'

// Is `aggregateName` the pre-erasure name of a BOXED union? Empty (the overwhelming majority — every
// parameter whose identity survived resolution) answers false without a registry probe, which keeps the
// `DeclaredEnum` this would otherwise box off the scalar path. Split out because the two functions above
// must ask it identically.
function asyncErasedUnionIsBoxed(signatures ProgramSignatures, aggregateName ByteArray) returns bool
	if aggregateName.isEmpty() 'identitySurvived'
		return false
	end 'identitySurvived'

	return match signatures.enumInAnyFile(aggregateName) 'e'
		declared(layout) gives layout.isBoxed()
		undeclared gives false
	end 'e'
end 'asyncErasedUnionIsBoxed'

// Collect every async callee spawned anywhere in the module, in first-seen source order, deduped. The walk
// mirrors `collectFnRefThunkTargets`' — one linear pass over the Maxon ops — and runs on the MAXON module,
// which the pipeline leaves intact.
//
// ⚠ It collects every spawned callee and NOT only the ones needing a releaser, because its caller runs
// BEFORE `noteDestructorUsage` and the admission test is cheap while a second walk would not be. The
// installer applies `asyncCalleeNeedsReleaser`.
export function collectAsyncSpawnTargets(module MaxonModule) returns ByteArrayArray
	var seen = AsyncReleaserSet.create()
	var targets = ByteArrayArray.create()
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
			let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("collectAsyncSpawnTargets: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
			for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
				noteIfAsyncSpawn(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectAsyncSpawnTargets: opIdx {opIdx} not in module.ops"), seen: seen, targets: targets)
			end 'eachOp'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return targets
end 'collectAsyncSpawnTargets'

function noteIfAsyncSpawn(op MaxonOp, seen AsyncReleaserSet, targets ByteArrayArray)
	match op 'op'
		asyncCall(_, callee, _, _, _, _, _) then noteAsyncCallee(callee, seen: seen, targets: targets)
		// Every other op names no SPAWNED callee, `await`/`tryAwait` included: the await side reaches its
		// releaser through the GT struct at run time, which is the whole point of storing the address there.
		// Two ranges rather than one because `asyncCall` sits mid-union, between `indirectCall` and `await`.
		call to indirectCall then break 'op'
		await to errorFlagEncode then break 'op'
		// A SERVICE's green thread owns its state and its request boxes and releases them in its own loop
		// (`ServiceLoop`), so it needs no per-callee releaser: the GT-struct release protocol this file
		// implements is the `async` PROMISE's, and a service has no promise.
		serviceSpawn to serviceReplyCell then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteIfAsyncSpawn'

function noteAsyncCallee(callee ByteArray, seen AsyncReleaserSet, targets ByteArrayArray)
	if seen.contains(callee) 'alreadySeen'
		return
	end 'alreadySeen'

	seen.insert(callee)
	targets.push(callee)
end 'noteAsyncCallee'

// ⭐⭐ **DECLARE WHAT THE SYNTHESIZED RELEASER BODIES WILL CALL — the obligation `noteDestructorUsage` states
// for the destructor cascades, owed here for the same reason and settled the same way.** A releaser's body
// is built AFTER the pipeline, so the Maxon-module scan that decides which runtime to install cannot see
// inside it: a releaser that calls `__str_decref` needs `usesStrDecref` turned on, and one that calls
// `__destruct_<T>` needs that destructor in `neededDestructors` BEFORE `closeDestructorNeeds` runs its
// fixpoint — otherwise the cascade the releaser names is never synthesized and the program dies at
// `resolveCallFixups: call to unknown function '__destruct_T'`, with no diagnostic and no line.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY BIT IS DERIVED FROM THE DROP CALLEE THE PLAN WILL ACTUALLY EMIT** — `declareDestructorCascadeNeeds`'
// own rule, and its header records what a hand-written roster of "the field kinds someone remembered" cost
// when it was wrong in all three arms at once. So this asks the plan, not the types.
//
// ⚠ Called BEFORE `noteDestructorUsage` so the destructors it roots are closed over transitively; a nested
// cascade is then the fixpoint's business and not this file's.
export function declareAsyncReleaserNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage, targets ByteArrayArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner)
	for callee in targets 'eachCallee'
		let plan = asyncReleasePlanFor(callee, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames)
		declareSlotDropNeeds(usage, slots: plan.argSlots)
		declareSlotDropNeeds(usage, slots: plan.resultSlots)
		declareSlotDropNeeds(usage, slots: plan.errorSlots)
	end 'eachCallee'
end 'declareAsyncReleaserNeeds'

// Each slot's drop callee declared through `RuntimeUsage.recordCallUsage` — the same routine the module scan
// runs over every call op — so a releaser's emitted call declares exactly what a hand-written call to the
// same entry would, and a new drop family needs no arm here.
function declareSlotDropNeeds(usage RuntimeUsage, slots AsyncManagedSlotArray)
	for slot in slots 'eachSlot'
		recordCallUsage(slot.dropCallee, usage: usage)
	end 'eachSlot'
end 'declareSlotDropNeeds'

// Install the releasers for every spawned callee that needs one. Appended already-lowered after the
// pipeline, exactly as the destructor cascades and the `__fnref_` thunks are. What runs over that appended
// band is `Compiler.compileToCodeResult`'s install block's to say, and is stated there once.
export function installAsyncReleasers(module StdModule, targets ByteArrayArray, funcSignatures FuncSignatureMap, signatures ProgramSignatures, typeNames TypeNameInterner)
	for callee in targets 'eachCallee'
		let plan = asyncReleasePlanFor(callee, funcSignatures: funcSignatures, signatures: signatures, typeNames: typeNames)
		if plan.isInert() 'nothingToRelease'
			continue
		end 'nothingToRelease'
		synthesizeReleaser(module, name: asyncReleaserName(callee), plan: plan)
	end 'eachCallee'
end 'installAsyncReleasers'

// `<name>(gt, resultConsumed)` — release this GT's managed arguments always, and its managed
// result-or-error only when `resultConsumed` is 0. See this file's header for which caller passes which and
// what each of the two mistakes costs.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME IS A PARAMETER BECAUSE TWO FAMILIES SHARE THIS BODY (SV2)** — `__gt_release_<callee>` for an
// `async` spawn and `__gt_reply_release_<message>` for a reply cell. They differ in their PLAN (see
// `serviceReplyReleasePlanFor` for the three facts that differ) and in nothing this function does, so a
// second copy of the guard order — arguments always, result under `resultConsumed`, success under
// `threw == 0` — would be the one place a wrong copy is a double free.
function synthesizeReleaser(module StdModule, name ByteArray, plan AsyncReleasePlan)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params(), returnLine: 0)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry")
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done")

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let gt = 0 as ValueId
	let resultConsumed = 1 as ValueId

	// The ARGUMENTS, unconditionally: the thread borrowed them whether it ran, parked or never started, and
	// the reference the spawn took is owed back on every one of those paths.
	var cur = emitSlotReleases(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, gt: gt, slots: plan.argSlots)

	if plan.resultSlots.count() == 0 and plan.errorSlots.count() == 0 'argsOnly'
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
		module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)
		module.functions.push(func)
		return
	end 'argsOnly'

	// `resultConsumed != 0` ⇒ an await already took the result's reference; stop here.
	var releaseResult = module.addBlock(func, label: b"releaseresult")
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let notConsumed = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: resultConsumed, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(notConsumed, thenBlockId: releaseResult.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	emitResultOrErrorRelease(module, func: func, ids: ids, start: releaseResult, done: done, gt: gt, plan: plan)

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'synthesizeReleaser'

// The RESULT-or-ERROR arm, reached only on the drop path.
//
// ⛔ **A NON-THROWING CALLEE'S `gt.threw` IS GARBAGE AND IS NEVER READ.** `buildGtTrampolineChunk` stores R10
// into it unconditionally and states that it is harmless *because only `__gt_try_await` reads it*. Branching
// on it for a non-throwing callee would turn that garbage into a control decision, and one of its two arms
// hands a garbage word to a drop callee.
//
// ⛔ **AND ON A THROW, `gt.result` IS THE UNDEFINED SUCCESS SLOT.** The trampoline captured R8 whatever the
// exit was, so on a throw it holds whatever the throwing path left there — releasing it would be a wild free.
// That is why the success release sits under `threw == 0` rather than beside the error release.
//
// A GT that never RAN has both words zero (the fresh slab zeroes, and `__gt_spawn`'s whole-struct memzero
// zeroes a recycled one), so every guard below falls through to `done` for it — which is the correct answer
// and the reason no `status` test is needed here.
function emitResultOrErrorRelease(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, start IrBlock, done IrBlock, gt ValueId, plan AsyncReleasePlan)
	if not plan.calleeThrows 'nonThrowing'
		module.setTerminator(emitSlotReleases(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: start, gt: gt, slots: plan.resultSlots), op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
		return
	end 'nonThrowing'

	var releaseSuccess = module.addBlock(func, label: b"releasesuccess")
	var releaseError = module.addBlock(func, label: b"releaseerror")
	let threw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(start, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(threw, addrId: gt, offset: GtOffThrew, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let noError = emitCmp(module, block: start, a: threw, b: emitConst(module, block: start, value: 0, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(start, op: StdOp.condBranch(noError, thenBlockId: releaseSuccess.id, elseBlockId: releaseError.id))

	module.setTerminator(emitSlotReleases(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: releaseSuccess, gt: gt, slots: plan.resultSlots), op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
	// `errorSlots` is empty for a throwing callee whose error type is a SCALAR clause — there is a `threw`
	// worth branching on and nothing in it to free — so this arm emits no drop at all and falls straight
	// through. That is the "callee throws" / "error is managed" split, and it is why they are two facts.
	module.setTerminator(emitSlotReleases(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: releaseError, gt: gt, slots: plan.errorSlots), op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
end 'emitResultOrErrorRelease'

// Drop each slot in turn, threading the continuation block — one `MmRuntime.emitNullGuardedDrop` per slot.
// Returns the block execution continues in.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THAT HELPER AND NOT A SECOND COPY OF IT.** A GT struct is a base address and a slot is a byte
// offset into it, which is exactly what `emitNullGuardedDrop` already takes; `FieldDropKind.direct` is
// exactly the release a slot's `dropCallee` wants. This loop therefore owns only the ITERATION.
//
// ⚠ The null guard is what makes a PARTIALLY INITIALIZED GT safe: `emitAsyncSpawn` fills the argument slots
// only after `__gt_spawn` returns, and the result/error words are written only by a thread that actually
// completed. A zero word means "nothing was ever put here", which is the one reading a release must not act
// on, and it is the reading a memzeroed recycled struct guarantees.
function emitSlotReleases(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, slots AsyncManagedSlotArray) returns IrBlock
	var block = cur
	for slot in slots 'eachSlot'
		block = emitNullGuardedDrop(module, cur: block, box: gt, offset: slot.offset, kind: FieldDropKind.direct(slot.dropCallee), ids: ids, func: func)
	end 'eachSlot'
	return block
end 'emitSlotReleases'
